soak-test: started 4 groups of one responder, 3 callers, 1 grinder and 1 tick waiter (24 user threads) on 4 online core(s), beating every 5s
soak-test: silence longer than a few beats is a hang, not a slow run; the beat is on the wall clock and does not depend on the workload making progress
soak-test: a clean run is a number to compare against, NOT evidence that the concurrency is correct (design/roadmap/0219-a-workload-that-does-not-stop.md)
soak-test: the threads that cross cores are the tick waiters, NOT the rendezvous pairs; a rising crossings count is the wake protocol sustained across cores under load, not the IPC workload migrating (design/roadmap/0221-a-soak-that-crosses-cores.md)
soak-test: rounds counts IPC round trips and wakes counts tick-route wakes; they are separate because they are separate quantities, and neither rate compares with a soak built differently
soak-test-reboot: THIS BUILD REBOOTS THE BOARD. It soaks for 120s, then asks for a cold reboot (SBI SRST system_reset, reset type 1) and draws the thread-placement lottery again, forever.

board-console: sending the soak escape to the board now: 1 byte, 0x0d. Draw 1 of 1 armed its reboot at +33.7s and this is the sample the series was asked for.
board-console: sent 1 byte, 0x0d. Waiting up to 15s for the kernel to say it found it.
soak-test-reboot: to stop the loop: press any key on this console. The check is a poll of the UART's data-ready bit once every 5s and again 5s before each reset, and the bit is sticky, so a keypress at any moment is found. Stopping disarms the reboot and leaves the soak running; it does not end the run.
soak-test-reboot: the fallback that needs no cooperation from this kernel is the firmware's own autoboot countdown or boot menu on the next boot, and after that, the boot medium.
soak-test-census: where the kernel placed each worker at spawn: R=responder, C=caller, G=grinder, W=tick waiter, and the number after each letter is its group
